      "seed": 1279802315,
      "version": 33,
      "versionNonce": 1983579881,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790692522346,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Cache -> Redis/Memcache",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Cache -> Redis/Memcache",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "HTdV3OLRNGNSOaHRhiv6W",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1855.4262467951967,
      "y": 22388.19981008637,
      "width": 1199,
      "height": 900,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bOb",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 388086277,
      "version": 2691,
      "versionNonce": 1678506023,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790692522346,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Do we even need a cache ?\n2. Do we need distributed cache?\n\nA good scaled instance of Redis lets say use something like Azure or AWS, A good scaled\ninstance of Redis can comfortabally handle few GBs of data\n\nElastic Amazon cache can help us to prepare Redis instance or memcache instance prepared\nwe can see here like\ncache.r6g.16xlarge machine having more than 400 GB of space and price of this is approx\n5$ per hour.\n\nPoint here is that, if the data that we want to store is probably like few GBs like 10 GB\nor 20 GB data then we can store that in a single instance pretty easily, we dont need to \nunnecessarily even have a sharded and distributed instance on Redis.\n\nLets say we want to design a Leaderboard where we have 1 Million concurrent users, if lets say\nwe have 1 million users for which we want to store the data. For 1M user, lets say the metadata \nthat we want to store is around 1 KB thats going to be only 1 GB of RAM. We dont need to unnecessarily\ndata distributed across nodes of Redis. This is very easily handlable by Redis.\n\n\n50M * 10KB -> 500 GB -> very less storage, using AWS hosted Mysql or Postgresql or Aurora we can use Terabyte\nof storage.\n\n\nThe point here is that we have good compute and storage capabilities available now a days in cloud\ninstances, if we are managing everything on ourselves then also we can get pretty heavy computable\nmachines but then we have to manage everything by ourselves. But unless that management is done by\nourself we no need to go there.\nwe will be talking about some numbers, this should set the context what do I want to say, that initially\nwe might think that storing 50 Million records might be too much but when we actually do the estimations\nwhat we actually calculate the number, what we actually realise is probably we will not need so much\namount of data 500GB is like nothing. A Iphone now a days has TB of storage available.\nUnless the storage is that we need to handle is too much like we start optimizing column oriented databases\n, we start optimizing sharding, xyz, first understand what do we need. Do we need something or we are\njust overprovisioning thing or over optimizing things.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Do we even need a cache ?\n2. Do we need distributed cache?\n\nA good scaled instance of Redis lets say use something like Azure or AWS, A good scaled\ninstance of Redis can comfortabally handle few GBs of data\n\nElastic Amazon cache can help us to prepare Redis instance or memcache instance prepared\nwe can see here like\ncache.r6g.16xlarge machine having more than 400 GB of space and price of this is approx\n5$ per hour.\n\nPoint here is that, if the data that we want to store is probably like few GBs like 10 GB\nor 20 GB data then we can store that in a single instance pretty easily, we dont need to \nunnecessarily even have a sharded and distributed instance on Redis.\n\nLets say we want to design a Leaderboard where we have 1 Million concurrent users, if lets say\nwe have 1 million users for which we want to store the data. For 1M user, lets say the metadata \nthat we want to store is around 1 KB thats going to be only 1 GB of RAM. We dont need to unnecessarily\ndata distributed across nodes of Redis. This is very easily handlable by Redis.\n\n\n50M * 10KB -> 500 GB -> very less storage, using AWS hosted Mysql or Postgresql or Aurora we can use Terabyte\nof storage.\n\n\nThe point here is that we have good compute and storage capabilities available now a days in cloud\ninstances, if we are managing everything on ourselves then also we can get pretty heavy computable\nmachines but then we have to manage everything by ourselves. But unless that management is done by\nourself we no need to go there.\nwe will be talking about some numbers, this should set the context what do I want to say, that initially\nwe might think that storing 50 Million records might be too much but when we actually do the estimations\nwhat we actually calculate the number, what we actually realise is probably we will not need so much\namount of data 500GB is like nothing. A Iphone now a days has TB of storage available.\nUnless the storage is that we need to handle is too much like we start optimizing column oriented databases\n, we start optimizing sharding, xyz, first understand what do we need. Do we need something or we are\njust overprovisioning thing or over optimizing things.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "hSZsiZAcu4iXpA8vZEJia",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1790.9731217951967,
      "y": 23372.22715383637,
      "width": 528,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bOc",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 346138973,
      "version": 83,
      "versionNonce": 753680841,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790692522346,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lets say we are provisioning Caching instance ->",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lets say we are provisioning Caching instance ->",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "MesVFsmkVDKOg-rJnQKEV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1871.5824967951967,
      "y": 23413.86387258637,
      "width": 1034,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bOd",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2066578845,
      "version": 1081,
      "versionNonce": 407395143,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790692522346,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "memory storage available -> 50 GB to 1 TB memory optimized instance.\nLatency -> Read -> ~1ms within the same region \n    Write -> 1-2 ms(mili seconds)\n    we can also improve latency by taking more powerful, faster instance.\n\n\nThroughput -> easily 100k req per second instance for aws elastic cache.\n\nSometimes a lot of interviews, interviewers talk about some very specific scenario, that\nwe have to design the system and the overall latency that we can maximum spend is like\n100 ms, we have to complete everything within 100 ms, then now its up to us, try to understand\nlets say ok, \nlets say we are doing database read on SSD and lets say our SLA is 100 ms, then a 10-20 ms\ndatabase read is going to be fine but lets say we have a very tight SLA lets say we have\nto complete full operation within 30 ms or 20 ms then probably we need to go to caches bcz\nthere the latency is going to be around 1 ms.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "memory storage available -> 50 GB to 1 TB memory optimized instance.\nLatency -> Read -> ~1ms within the same region \n    Write -> 1-2 ms(mili seconds)\n    we can also improve latency by taking more powerful, faster instance.\n\n\nThroughput -> easily 100k req per second instance for aws elastic cache.\n\nSometimes a lot of interviews, interviewers talk about some very specific scenario, that\nwe have to design the system and the overall latency that we can maximum spend is like\n100 ms, we have to complete everything within 100 ms, then now its up to us, try to understand\nlets say ok, \nlets say we are doing database read on SSD and lets say our SLA is 100 ms, then a 10-20 ms\ndatabase read is going to be fine but lets say we have a very tight SLA lets say we have\nto complete full operation within 30 ms or 20 ms then probably we need to go to caches bcz\nthere the latency is going to be around 1 ms.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "-cgY5OJCec_f06m-plNsG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1871.9692155451967,
      "y": 23850.00840383637,
      "width": 297,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bOf",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 303624221,
      "version": 31,
      "versionNonce": 863183017,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790692522346,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When to Shard our Cache -> ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When to Shard our Cache -> ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "qvkdbMMNAg8Ixyt-VbBFM",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1906.3403092951967,
      "y": 23878.27012258637,
      "width": 1089,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bOg",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1855905843,
      "version": 323,
      "versionNonce": 1229675111,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790692522346,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "If our data is going beyound 500GB or 1 TB, then we should start thinking about cachin mechanism\nand lets say bcz of our load, our read latency is increasing because our overall load is increasing\nthen may be bit of horizontal scaling is useful.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "If our data is going beyound 500GB or 1 TB, then we should start thinking about cachin mechanism\nand lets say bcz of our load, our read latency is increasing because our overall load is increasing\nthen may be bit of horizontal scaling is useful.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "h5gsii-TKnTXDn2e7lNs9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1830.6664811701967,
      "y": 24059.10606008637,
      "width": 528,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bOj",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 478119310,
      "version": 67,
      "versionNonce": 1199252361,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790692522346,
      "created": 1789651241946,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "We had to design a badging system for employees?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "We had to design a badging system for employees?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "b2ycDtOfKQ533dqbZJws4",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1867.4242936701967,
      "y": 24097.68027883637,
      "width": 1155,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bOk",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1750146062,
      "version": 1866,
      "versionNonce": 1541502343,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790692522346,
      "created": 1789651290126,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "lets say 100 K employees, Badging system needs to be very fast, like the moment we tap it immediately\ntells, whethere we are able to authenticaate or not.\n\nwhat will be peak load coming from these 100 thousand employees, All will not badge in at same time\nobviously, lets say everyone coming into office, then also everybody will not badge in the same time,\nbadge in technically limited by the number of badge in machines, Lets say we have 10 offices, And in each\noffice we have 2 badge in machines, So at any point of time maximum 20 badge in will come at max.\nso 20 is peak load in our system.\nOnce we realize we need to do 20 badge per second max, then now we have to figure out the SLA, Now this\nSLA part may be our interviewer will give, we can ask interviewer how fast you want system will respond\nto us. Then our interviewer will say lets say overall latency is < 1 sec. Now within 1 sec we want to \nknow whether the user is able to authenticate or not. Now we will start figuring out that where we need\nto store the data. Because the employee data is the data that doesn't very frequently change. Once data\nhas been written it doesn't change alot.\nBecause the scenario we computed, we realize that we need to store the data in a cache, then already\nread on the employee data is going to be around 1 ms and we have 1 sec of SLA. Like within a sec we want\nthe system to respond.\nwe just need to check badgeId is valid or not, we can do it in redis easily within 1 ms, now we have to \nfigure out where else the time will go, may be time will go in cryptographic conversions",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "lets say 100 K employees, Badging system needs to be very fast, like the moment we tap it immediately\ntells, whethere we are able to authenticaate or not.\n\nwhat will be peak load coming from these 100 thousand employees, All will not badge in at same time\nobviously, lets say everyone coming into office, then also everybody will not badge in the same time,\nbadge in technically limited by the number of badge in machines, Lets say we have 10 offices, And in each\noffice we have 2 badge in machines, So at any point of time maximum 20 badge in will come at max.\nso 20 is peak load in our system.\nOnce we realize we need to do 20 badge per second max, then now we have to figure out the SLA, Now this\nSLA part may be our interviewer will give, we can ask interviewer how fast you want system will respond\nto us. Then our interviewer will say lets say overall latency is < 1 sec. Now within 1 sec we want to \nknow whether the user is able to authenticate or not. Now we will start figuring out that where we need\nto store the data. Because the employee data is the data that doesn't very frequently change. Once data\nhas been written it doesn't change alot.\nBecause the scenario we computed, we realize that we need to store the data in a cache, then already\nread on the employee data is going to be around 1 ms and we have 1 sec of SLA. Like within a sec we want\nthe system to respond.\nwe just need to check badgeId is valid or not, we can do it in redis easily within 1 ms, now we have to \nfigure out where else the time will go, may be time will go in cryptographic conversions",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "6UguWfSwm7xf7bIAZTAAN",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1750.6664811701967,
      "y": 24711.10606008637,
      "width": 132,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
