      "text": "Databases ->",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Databases ->",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "zxAedv0_7O3oC_HCeOy89",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1828.6664811701967,
      "y": 24738.97324758637,
      "width": 979,
      "height": 50,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bOn",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2101440914,
      "version": 119,
      "versionNonce": 1732717735,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790692522346,
      "created": 1789652504517,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lets say we want to store data in databases, then what kind of storage capacity we should\nuse?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lets say we want to store data in databases, then what kind of storage capacity we should\nuse?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "9OC6gdpLVRjaV85lI1ZVn",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1876.6703874201967,
      "y": 24802.03574758637,
      "width": 1056,
      "height": 625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bOp",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2145758030,
      "version": 1360,
      "versionNonce": 1972147529,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790692522346,
      "created": 1789652572387,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Most of the systems are somehow supported by SQL, assuming this we can initially think\nSQL system to start with like MySQL and PgSQL.\nFew Terra Byte (TB) of data we should be able to very easily handled\n\nSQL RDS -> 64TB we can easily go with RDS, if we want to extend it to 128TB then we can\n        go with Amazon Aurora.\n\nBut in a nutshell a few TB of data should not bother us to store in a single instance,\nthis everything we are talking about single instance. no horizontally scaled or sharded\nor partitioned data.\n\nLatency & Throughput of read and write in MYSQL RDS instances maximum ->\nLatency -> Read latency around 5-30 ms, considering we are not doing n+1 query\n    write -> 5-30 ms\n\nThroughput -> upto 50K transactions persecond for read, 20K transactions per second for \n    writes\n\n-> If storage is beyond 50 TB or 100 TB then we can consider sharding.\n\n\n-> if we want to reduce latency for reads , then good strategy would be cross region replication\n    if we want sub mili second latency then cachine we can think of.\n\nSo if we have few 100 GBs of data, dont start sharding unnecessarily.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Most of the systems are somehow supported by SQL, assuming this we can initially think\nSQL system to start with like MySQL and PgSQL.\nFew Terra Byte (TB) of data we should be able to very easily handled\n\nSQL RDS -> 64TB we can easily go with RDS, if we want to extend it to 128TB then we can\n        go with Amazon Aurora.\n\nBut in a nutshell a few TB of data should not bother us to store in a single instance,\nthis everything we are talking about single instance. no horizontally scaled or sharded\nor partitioned data.\n\nLatency & Throughput of read and write in MYSQL RDS instances maximum ->\nLatency -> Read latency around 5-30 ms, considering we are not doing n+1 query\n    write -> 5-30 ms\n\nThroughput -> upto 50K transactions persecond for read, 20K transactions per second for \n    writes\n\n-> If storage is beyond 50 TB or 100 TB then we can consider sharding.\n\n\n-> if we want to reduce latency for reads , then good strategy would be cross region replication\n    if we want sub mili second latency then cachine we can think of.\n\nSo if we have few 100 GBs of data, dont start sharding unnecessarily.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "qwMj4WnjdgnQEEGZbDVPo",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1742.7836686701967,
      "y": 25528.34043508637,
      "width": 572,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bOr",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1933929742,
      "version": 117,
      "versionNonce": 1730740167,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790692522346,
      "created": 1789653515428,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When to Horizontaly scale our application service ->",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When to Horizontaly scale our application service ->",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "6wfcy82hfiG2IjPvHuz67",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1832.6313249201967,
      "y": 25563.46934133637,
      "width": 1155,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bOs",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 809068917,
      "version": 892,
      "versionNonce": 1002243113,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790692522346,
      "created": 1790342404007,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lets say we have deployed our application servers on AWS EC2, where our servers actually running, howmany\nconcurrent max request a single EC2 can support -> \n100 K requests are very easily handled by a single instance\n\nIf lets say we want to create a stateful system, considering that we want to store some data in memory\nRAM -> 100-500 GBs in standard machine not much so large.\n\nstartup time -> on horizontal scaling we can assume 30 to 60 sec to start a new EC2 machine.\n\nwhen we horizontally scale\n - when our CPU/Memory utilization beyond 70% -> it is good time to horizontaly scale\n - if response latency is beyond our SLA then also try to horizontally scale.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lets say we have deployed our application servers on AWS EC2, where our servers actually running, howmany\nconcurrent max request a single EC2 can support -> \n100 K requests are very easily handled by a single instance\n\nIf lets say we want to create a stateful system, considering that we want to store some data in memory\nRAM -> 100-500 GBs in standard machine not much so large.\n\nstartup time -> on horizontal scaling we can assume 30 to 60 sec to start a new EC2 machine.\n\nwhen we horizontally scale\n - when our CPU/Memory utilization beyond 70% -> it is good time to horizontaly scale\n - if response latency is beyond our SLA then also try to horizontally scale.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "uC_HzuNySoDpYbp-8hDEA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1740.6664811701967,
      "y": 26035.10606008637,
      "width": 187,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bOt",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 882606357,
      "version": 29,
      "versionNonce": 765685479,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790692522346,
      "created": 1790343152932,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Message Queues ->",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Message Queues ->",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "2sZpq7srfQMgU6ZNm9dTN",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1800.2992936701967,
      "y": 26085.89121633637,
      "width": 1089,
      "height": 750,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bOv",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1799221403,
      "version": 1626,
      "versionNonce": 62691081,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790692522346,
      "created": 1790343168196,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "AWS SQS -> Normal SQL can handle 3000 messages per second very easily\n\nRabbitMQ -> Throughput -> <30K messages per second easily without optimization\n    Latency -> 1-10 mili seconds\n\nSo if we have to process Thousands of message per second RabbitMQ or SQS is fine to work\n\nKafka (Streaming service) -> \n    Throughput -> Kafka Provides the highest throughput of all systems, writing 15x faster than\n            RabbitMQ and 2x faster than pulsar\n            500K msg/sec\n\n    Latency -> Kafka provides the lowest latency at higher throughputs, while also providing\n        strong durability and high availability\n        RabbitMQ can achieve lower latency than kafka but only at significantly lower throughputs.\n        1-5ms -> endto end latency from producer to consumer.\n\n    msg size -> 1KB to 4-5MB is easily handled by kafka.\n    we can keep few months of data.\n\n\nWhen we consider sharding kafka ->\n    when we want the data available in multiple regions or \n    throughtput or latency is reaching peak or SLA that might be a good time to shard\n\n\nKafkakorma is a real-time API management platform designed to explore Apache kafka streams directly\nto web, mobile or IOT devices.\n\nwe can say in interview these all numbers coming from pricing and providioning page of AWS.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "AWS SQS -> Normal SQL can handle 3000 messages per second very easily\n\nRabbitMQ -> Throughput -> <30K messages per second easily without optimization\n    Latency -> 1-10 mili seconds\n\nSo if we have to process Thousands of message per second RabbitMQ or SQS is fine to work\n\nKafka (Streaming service) -> \n    Throughput -> Kafka Provides the highest throughput of all systems, writing 15x faster than\n            RabbitMQ and 2x faster than pulsar\n            500K msg/sec\n\n    Latency -> Kafka provides the lowest latency at higher throughputs, while also providing\n        strong durability and high availability\n        RabbitMQ can achieve lower latency than kafka but only at significantly lower throughputs.\n        1-5ms -> endto end latency from producer to consumer.\n\n    msg size -> 1KB to 4-5MB is easily handled by kafka.\n    we can keep few months of data.\n\n\nWhen we consider sharding kafka ->\n    when we want the data available in multiple regions or \n    throughtput or latency is reaching peak or SLA that might be a good time to shard\n\n\nKafkakorma is a real-time API management platform designed to explore Apache kafka streams directly\nto web, mobile or IOT devices.\n\nwe can say in interview these all numbers coming from pricing and providioning page of AWS.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
