      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lets say we have 1 million daily active users, all of these users dont sort urls daily, most\nof them are going to use the urls. this is the first thing that we are going to take in\nconsideration during interview, like what we will get more like we will get more read\noperatoins or more write operations, hence it is a read heavy system.\n\n1 Million users -> 50% daily creat new short url\n        on average lets say 1 user create 2 short urls per day.\n\nNo of short urls we are creating daily ->  1 million per day\n\n\n10^6 writes per day \n\n84600 sec in a day\n\nno of writes per second ~ 10^6/10^5 => 10 writes per second\n\nlets assume read is 10x\nread per second(throughput) -> 100\n\nat peak time some kind of sale or something lets say -> 5x\nwps -> 50\nrps -> 500\n\n\nwe have considered 1 million urls per day , so we need to figure out howmuch storage we\ntechnically need\n\nFor every url lets say atleast apart from basic metadata, we will store the main url and the\nshortened url, this is the point which we dont know immediately, depending on our data\nmodeling we will decide how we will store data, if we have not done data modeling then its\nhard to do storage data.\n\nBut this looks like a straight forward mechanism where we atleast need to store main url and\nshortened url\n\nlets consider avg len of main url -> 100 char\n                         short url -> 10 char\n            110 Bytes ~ 100 Bytes\n\nPer day storage required -> 1 Million * 100 Bytes\n                        -> 0.1 Billion Bytes -> 0.1 * 10^9 Bytes ~ 0.1 GB per day\n                        -> 1024 ~ KB\n                        -> 10^6 ~ MB\n                        -> 10^9 ~ GB\n\ndata in an year -> 365 * 0.1 GB ~ 40 GB per year\nfor next 10 years -> 400 GB storage\n\nSO do we need to shard our database ? -> No Single database instance is fine\n\nlets say 10% increament every year\n40 + 44 + 48 + 53 + ......\n~ 400-500 GBs only\n~ Single instance\n\nWhat else we can figure out, if we are storing things in a single instance\nlatency ->Read 5-10 ms, Write->5-30 ms\n\nfrom here we need to figure out how we want to short our url, what kind of algo we want to\npick, howmuch time it will take and so on. and based on that we will figure out we want to\ncache some urls to reduce read latency or read load in DB. Like these are very frequently\nused URLs we can cache it. So this is the starting point.\n\n\nSometime what can happen is our interviewer can say you have 1 Billion users as daily active\nusers, then we have to do calculations based on that.",
      "fontSize": 19.481249999999996,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lets say we have 1 million daily active users, all of these users dont sort urls daily, most of them are going to use the urls. this is the first thing that we are going to take in consideration during interview, like what we will get more like we will get more read operatoins or more write operations, hence it is a read heavy system.\n\n1 Million users -> 50% daily creat new short url\n        on average lets say 1 user create 2 short urls per day.\n\nNo of short urls we are creating daily ->  1 million per day\n\n\n10^6 writes per day \n\n84600 sec in a day\n\nno of writes per second ~ 10^6/10^5 => 10 writes per second\n\nlets assume read is 10x\nread per second(throughput) -> 100\n\nat peak time some kind of sale or something lets say -> 5x\nwps -> 50\nrps -> 500\n\n\nwe have considered 1 million urls per day , so we need to figure out howmuch storage we technically need\n\nFor every url lets say atleast apart from basic metadata, we will store the main url and the\nshortened url, this is the point which we dont know immediately, depending on our data modeling we will decide how we will store data, if we have not done data modeling then its\nhard to do storage data.\n\nBut this looks like a straight forward mechanism where we atleast need to store main url and shortened url\n\nlets consider avg len of main url -> 100 char\n                         short url -> 10 char\n            110 Bytes ~ 100 Bytes\n\nPer day storage required -> 1 Million * 100 Bytes\n                        -> 0.1 Billion Bytes -> 0.1 * 10^9 Bytes ~ 0.1 GB per day\n                        -> 1024 ~ KB\n                        -> 10^6 ~ MB\n                        -> 10^9 ~ GB\n\ndata in an year -> 365 * 0.1 GB ~ 40 GB per year\nfor next 10 years -> 400 GB storage\n\nSO do we need to shard our database ? -> No Single database instance is fine\n\nlets say 10% increament every year\n40 + 44 + 48 + 53 + ......\n~ 400-500 GBs only\n~ Single instance\n\nWhat else we can figure out, if we are storing things in a single instance\nlatency ->Read 5-10 ms, Write->5-30 ms\n\nfrom here we need to figure out how we want to short our url, what kind of algo we want to pick, howmuch time it will take and so on. and based on that we will figure out we want to cache some urls to reduce read latency or read load in DB. Like these are very frequently used URLs we can cache it. So this is the starting point.\n\n\nSometime what can happen is our interviewer can say you have 1 Billion users as daily active users, then we have to do calculations based on that.",
