    {
      "id": "zWYJGrqJb2DCEGg7SJgd_",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1917.3735124201967,
      "y": 21249.67246633637,
      "width": 1025.20703125,
      "height": 600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bP8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1579850567,
      "version": 1992,
      "versionNonce": 268846167,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790778487054,
      "created": 1790692876941,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "It is a very simple piece of software it takes urls like www.google.com and what it gives\nsomething like bit.ly/8ec, bit.ly is stay as it is, 8ec is technically\nshortened form of www.google.com, for this it looks something odd due to it is already pretty\nshort, but think about links where we have google docs, these are\nvery long. think about sharing some youtube playlist, think about very long image\nurls and so on. In these scenarios sharing url becomes bit of hectic task.\nCompanies like Google, LinkedIn, Microsoft everywhere they have internally their own URL\nshortener.\nWhere lets say we have to share a design doc, we dont need to share a very long link, we can\njust type unique url 8ec, there is a functionality to create custom \nunique URLs as well.\nIts not just URL shortener needed for making URLs small or making URLs handy, it has got more\nwider use cases as well\n\nLets say we want to add an add Campaign and we want to figure out, where exactly the traffic\nis coming from, Lets say we have our website www.supercake.com, lets say some people are\nredirecting here from youtube, some are from google, maybe some from insta and all, we want\nto track where the people are actually coming from, URL shortener can be handy for those\ncases as well. for google lets say www.supercake.com?key=google and for other other key is\nthere so we will take diff diff key value pair and short them, so we have diff shortened url\nfor our website.\nwe can use Shortened URL in multiple ways.\n\nHow exactly in interviews or we need to make our own service, How we can actually make it?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "It is a very simple piece of software it takes urls like www.google.com and what it gives something like bit.ly/8ec, bit.ly is stay as it is, 8ec is technically\nshortened form of www.google.com, for this it looks something odd due to it is already pretty short, but think about links where we have google docs, these are\nvery long. think about sharing some youtube playlist, think about very long image\nurls and so on. In these scenarios sharing url becomes bit of hectic task.\nCompanies like Google, LinkedIn, Microsoft everywhere they have internally their own URL shortener.\nWhere lets say we have to share a design doc, we dont need to share a very long link, we can just type unique url 8ec, there is a functionality to create custom \nunique URLs as well.\nIts not just URL shortener needed for making URLs small or making URLs handy, it has got more wider use cases as well\n\nLets say we want to add an add Campaign and we want to figure out, where exactly the traffic is coming from, Lets say we have our website www.supercake.com, lets say some people are redirecting here from youtube, some are from google, maybe some from insta and all, we want to track where the people are actually coming from, URL shortener can be handy for those cases as well. for google lets say www.supercake.com?key=google and for other other key is there so we will take diff diff key value pair and short them, so we have diff shortened url for our website.\nwe can use Shortened URL in multiple ways.\n\nHow exactly in interviews or we need to make our own service, How we can actually make it?",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "rXwNUKvmJVjwDHtIzzUaz",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1904.4399186701967,
      "y": 21977.85996633637,
      "width": 451,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bP9",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1740351065,
      "version": 46,
      "versionNonce": 849109433,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790776003129,
      "created": 1790775991729,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What are the major problems we can face ?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What are the major problems we can face ?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "-YHM9nztMsgWOrkcCwFtR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1932.7172624201967,
      "y": 22019.27402883637,
      "width": 1040.25,
      "height": 1500,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bPA",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1500110263,
      "version": 3639,
      "versionNonce": 1255892247,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790778478903,
      "created": 1790776075945,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Storage of URLs\n2. Algo -> How would we like to short our URL\n3. Working Mechanism\n\nLets try to figure out some numbers to start with \n\nFirstly everytime try to think that what is the business usecase means when user will use our\nplateform what they are going to use? here we talk about what the system is expected to be\ndone, thats what we are going to refer as Functional requirement, tip here is that always come\nup with the functional requirements that we can complete in 1 hour or 45 minute interview,\nalways make functional requirements crisp,short\n\n1.  user should be able to come up on the plateform, give an original url and get a shortened\nversion of it\n2. whenever anyone open the shortened url, they should be redirected to original url\n\nHere we will discuss more ideas but depending on time we will restrict ourselves during\ninterview, for 45 minutes above 2 are more than enough\n\n3 user can give a custom short string, and if that short string is not already used, we can\nalllocate that to url\n\nbefore moving to non functional requirements we can ask interviewer, howmany users we have in\nthe plateform, this is something that business team will tell us, lets say we have 1 Million\nmonthly active users.\n\n1M MAU(Monthly active users)\n\n50% of MAU will create on average 2 short urls daily, means daily we need to support 1 Million\nshortened urls\n\n1 M urls daily -> in 1 years 400 Million of urls will be created -> peak avg -> 500 Million of\nurls for year\n\nnext 20 year -> 500 * 20 -> 10000 Million -> 10 Billion urls\n\nif there will be 10K MAU -> 10K URLs daily -> 10K * 400 -> 4000 K urls year\nnext 20 years -> 4000K & 20 -> 80 M urls so there is a huge difference of numbers according to\ndaily active users. The moment no of users change, a lot of things change\n\nif we will assume by ourself, scaling may become very hard so usually what we do in interview\nwe will ask to interviewer what number we can assume as monthly active users, if they are not\ngiving any number then we will start probably with small numbers like 100 thousand users or\n100 K users or may be 10K, because not everyone has scale of instagram or netflix.\nusers but lot of time, interviews give opinion on howmany monthly active users we have.\n\n45:00\n\n\nNow we need to setup Non Functional Requirement here not only Product Manager work,here\nengineering Leader will also be involved\nSLA(Service Level Agreement) -> contract between service provider and customer\n\nNon Functional requirement here we talk about technical aspect what our system will support,\nwhat will be the scaling factors, howmuch reliable our services and so on, so it more like\nquality attributes, sometime we say our system will be highly scalable, this is a very vague\nterm, we will not talk like this, so here in non functional requirement we talk with the help\nof numbers if possible.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Storage of URLs\n2. Algo -> How would we like to short our URL\n3. Working Mechanism\n\nLets try to figure out some numbers to start with \n\nFirstly everytime try to think that what is the business usecase means when user will use our plateform what they are going to use? here we talk about what the system is expected to be done, thats what we are going to refer as Functional requirement, tip here is that always come up with the functional requirements that we can complete in 1 hour or 45 minute interview, always make functional requirements crisp,short\n\n1.  user should be able to come up on the plateform, give an original url and get a shortened version of it\n2. whenever anyone open the shortened url, they should be redirected to original url\n\nHere we will discuss more ideas but depending on time we will restrict ourselves during interview, for 45 minutes above 2 are more than enough\n\n3 user can give a custom short string, and if that short string is not already used, we can alllocate that to url\n\nbefore moving to non functional requirements we can ask interviewer, howmany users we have in the plateform, this is something that business team will tell us, lets say we have 1 Million monthly active users.\n\n1M MAU(Monthly active users)\n\n50% of MAU will create on average 2 short urls daily, means daily we need to support 1 Million shortened urls\n\n1 M urls daily -> in 1 years 400 Million of urls will be created -> peak avg -> 500 Million of urls for year\n\nnext 20 year -> 500 * 20 -> 10000 Million -> 10 Billion urls\n\nif there will be 10K MAU -> 10K URLs daily -> 10K * 400 -> 4000 K urls year\nnext 20 years -> 4000K & 20 -> 80 M urls so there is a huge difference of numbers according to daily active users. The moment no of users change, a lot of things change\n\nif we will assume by ourself, scaling may become very hard so usually what we do in interview\nwe will ask to interviewer what number we can assume as monthly active users, if they are not giving any number then we will start probably with small numbers like 100 thousand users or 100 K users or may be 10K, because not everyone has scale of instagram or netflix.\nusers but lot of time, interviews give opinion on howmany monthly active users we have.\n\n45:00\n\n\nNow we need to setup Non Functional Requirement here not only Product Manager work,here engineering Leader will also be involved\nSLA(Service Level Agreement) -> contract between service provider and customer\n\nNon Functional requirement here we talk about technical aspect what our system will support, what will be the scaling factors, howmuch reliable our services and so on, so it more like\nquality attributes, sometime we say our system will be highly scalable, this is a very vague term, we will not talk like this, so here in non functional requirement we talk with the help of numbers if possible.\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
